{"author":{"id":"c7109691837125ce179ae67c00dda2acb755e6dfb3646ed3e9aad234d639bbcc"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1775652617,"nonce":"vUBmvvgTqFg7zqfQdi/JiyNrrrY=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs77zqhf","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/18","origin":"github"},"title":"Migration from Jax-FEM to FEAX","message":"# Migrating from JAX-FEM to FEAX\n\nWe want to evaluate migrating the jNO FEM backend from **JAX-FEM** to **FEAX**.\n\nThe motivation is mainly:\n\n- cleaner integration with the JAX-native traced workflow in jNO,\n- fewer limitations in the current JAX-FEM route,\n- and better support for time-dependent FEM workflows moving forward.\n\nA key technical reason is that **JAX-FEM relies on PETSc for assembly**, so the assembly path is not fully inside JAX. This makes integration with JAX-native solver and optimization libraries harder, especially for workflows where we want the full FEM pipeline to remain differentiable and composable inside the traced setup. In particular, using JAX-based solvers such as **Optimistix** becomes more difficult in the current setup.\n\n**FEAX**, in contrast, is fully JAX-based and differentiable, which makes it a better fit for jNO's symbolic and compiled workflow. This could also open the door to more natural **solver-in-the-loop** and **fully differentiable simulation** workflows in the future.\n\nThis issue is meant to track the technical reasons for the migration, expected benefits, and compatibility checks for weak-form assembly and boundary handling.","files":null}]}